query:
[Bug]: browserContext.storageState() runs third-party page JS and can fail when a visited origin has a service worker

### Version

1.63.0

### Steps to reproduce

`browserContext.storageState()` collects `localStorage` by navigating a throwaway page to every visited origin. That navigation is meant to be inert — the page gets a prepended request interceptor that fulfills every request with an empty document. But request interception does not apply to responses served by a service worker, so for an origin with an active worker the site's own JS runs during the capture. If that JS navigates (a signed-out app redirecting to its sign-in page is the common case), the redirect aborts the capture and `storageState()` rejects.

This script is self-contained: two local origins, the first one installing a worker whose `fetch` handler serves a shell that redirects on load.

```js
import http from 'node:http'

import { chromium } from 'playwright'

// Origin A: a site that installs a service worker. The worker serves a cached app shell for
// navigations to "/", and that shell redirects — what a signed-out app commonly does.
const shell =
  '<html><body>SHELL<script>addEventListener("load", () => { location.href = "/signed-out" })</scr' +
  'ipt></body></html>'
const a = http.createServer((req, res) => {
  const { pathname } = new URL(req.url, 'http://x')
  if (pathname === '/sw.js')
    return res.writeHead(200, { 'content-type': 'text/javascript' }).end(`
      self.addEventListener('install', (e) => e.waitUntil(self.skipWaiting()))
      self.addEventListener('activate', (e) => e.waitUntil(self.clients.claim()))
      self.addEventListener('fetch', (event) => {
        if (event.request.mode !== 'navigate' || new URL(event.request.url).pathname !== '/') return
        event.respondWith(new Response(${JSON.stringify(shell)}, { headers: { 'content-type': 'text/html' } }))
      })`)
  if (pathname === '/signed-out') return res.writeHead(200, { 'content-type': 'text/html' }).end('<html>bye</html>')
  res.writeHead(200, { 'content-type': 'text/html' }).end(
    '<html><body><script>localStorage.setItem("a", "1"); navigator.serviceWorker.register("/sw.js")</script></body></html>',
  )
})
// Origin B: any second origin the context also visited.
const b = http.createServer((_req, res) =>
  res
    .writeHead(200, { 'content-type': 'text/html' })
    .end('<html><body><script>localStorage.setItem("b", "1")</script></body></html>'),
)
await new Promise((r) => a.listen(0, '127.0.0.1', r))
await new Promise((r) => b.listen(0, '127.0.0.1', r))
const A = `http://localhost:${a.address().port}`
const B = `http://localhost:${b.address().port}`

const browser = await chromium.launch()
// Pass { serviceWorkers: 'block' } here and storageState() succeeds.
const context = await browser.newContext()
const page = await context.newPage()

await page.goto(A) // installs the worker
// wait for the worker to control the origin (resolves immediately; never resolves when workers are blocked)
await page.evaluate(() =>
  Promise.race([navigator.serviceWorker.ready.then(() => undefined), new Promise((resolve) => setTimeout(resolve, 3000))]),
)
await page.goto(B)
await page.goto('about:blank') // leave both origins, so storageState() must visit them itself

try {
  const state = await context.storageState()
  console.log('storageState OK, origins =', JSON.stringify(state.origins.map((o) => o.origin)))
} catch (error) {
  console.log('storageState FAILED:', error.message.split('\n')[0])
}

await browser.close()
a.close()
b.close()
```

### Expected behavior

`storageState()` returns the `localStorage` of both origins without running either origin's code — which is what the empty-document interceptor on the internal page is there to guarantee.

```
storageState OK, origins = ["http://localhost:50369","http://localhost:50370"]
```

That is what happens with `newContext({ serviceWorkers: 'block' })` (2 runs out of 2), and the `localStorage` of both origins is still captured correctly.

### Actual behavior

The worker serves the probe navigation, the shell's `load` handler redirects, and the capture is aborted (3 runs out of 3):

```
storageState FAILED: browserContext.storageState: Execution context was destroyed, most likely because of a navigation
```


This is Chromium-specific. Running the same script on Firefox and WebKit, the worker activates and serves navigations there too (a second `page.goto(A)` returns the worker's shell), yet `storageState()` succeeds on both — so their capture paths are not exposed to service workers, only Chromium's is.


### Possible fix

Make the internal storage-state page genuinely inert by bypassing service workers for it, rather than relying only on request interception. Chromium has `Network.setBypassServiceWorker`, which does not appear to be used anywhere in the codebase. I verified the mechanism from the outside on a page I own: with a `page.route('**/*')` fulfilling every request, the active worker still served its own redirecting content, while adding

```js
const session = await context.newCDPSession(page)
await session.send('Network.enable')
await session.send('Network.setBypassServiceWorker', { bypass: true })
```

made the route win: the same navigation got the route's document and no longer redirected.

Since Firefox and WebKit are not affected, fixing the Chromium probe would resolve the whole issue.

### Workarounds today, for anyone landing here

All three make `storageState()` succeed with every origin's `localStorage` intact (each verified 3 runs out of 3 on 1.63.0).

**1. Block service workers for the whole context.** One line, but it changes the browser the tests run in rather than only the internal probe — the site under test (or the third party) no longer behaves as in production:

```js
const context = await browser.newContext({ serviceWorkers: 'block' })
```

**2. Unregister the workers via CDP, just before the capture.** Workers stay enabled while the tests run; they are only removed once the context is about to be captured. Relies on `context.serviceWorkers()` and on the default scope being the script's directory:

```js
const cdp = await context.newCDPSession(page)
await cdp.send('ServiceWorker.enable')
for (const worker of context.serviceWorkers())
  await cdp.send('ServiceWorker.unregister', { scopeURL: new URL('.', worker.url()).href })
await cdp.send('ServiceWorker.stopAllWorkers')
await cdp.detach()

const state = await context.storageState()
```

**3. Unregister the workers from inside the page, just before the capture.** Same timing as 2, no CDP — only web-standard APIs, so it also works where `newCDPSession` is unavailable. A throwaway page visits each origin the context has seen (the caller has to track those): the route keeps origins without a worker off the network entirely, and the init script unregisters registrations at document start, before the worker-served shell's own scripts can redirect:

```js
const cleanupPage = await context.newPage()
await cleanupPage.route('**/*', (route) => route.fulfill({ body: '<html></html>', contentType: 'text/html' }))
await cleanupPage.addInitScript(() => {
  navigator.serviceWorker
    ?.getRegistrations()
    .then((registrations) => Promise.all(registrations.map((registration) => registration.unregister())))
})
for (const origin of visitedOrigins) {
  await cleanupPage.goto(origin, { waitUntil: 'commit' }).catch(() => undefined)
  // the worker-served page may still navigate once; waitForFunction survives that and
  // settles once a document confirms no registration is left
  await cleanupPage.waitForFunction(async () => (await navigator.serviceWorker.getRegistrations()).length === 0)
}
await cleanupPage.close()

const state = await context.storageState()
```

### Environment

- Playwright 1.63.0 (bundled Chrome Headless Shell 153.0.8010.12)
- Node v24.18.1
- macOS 26.6.2, arm64

---

*Investigated and written with LLM assistance (Claude Code). The repro script above was run on the environment listed here — 3 failing runs with the default context, 2 passing runs with `serviceWorkers: 'block'` — and the `Network.setBypassServiceWorker` finding was verified the same way rather than inferred.*

