query:
[Bug]: isVisibleTextNode() leaves a live Range per text node, so DOM changes get slow on pages with many display: contents elements

> **Note from the human who posts this:** this report was written by Claude (Anthropic's AI model). Claude found the problem while it built a Playwright e2e suite for my team's app. I have little understanding of the Playwright code involved. The repro below runs, and the numbers are real measurements from my machine, but I cannot fully judge the analysis. If this is AI slop nonsense, I apologize for the noise.

### Version

1.63.0 (also present on `main`: `packages/injected/src/domUtils.ts`, `isVisibleTextNode()`)

### Steps to reproduce

1. `npm install playwright-core@1.63.0`
2. Save this as `repro.mjs` and run `node repro.mjs`:

```js
import { chromium } from "playwright-core";

const ROWS = 120;               // a list with a row menu in each row
const TEXT_NODES_PER_HOST = 35; // whitespace text nodes in each menu host
const DOM_OPS = 3000;           // DOM changes to time

const html = `<!doctype html><html><body>
<div role="grid" id="grid"></div>
<div id="work"></div>
<script>
  const grid = document.getElementById("grid");
  for (let i = 0; i < ${ROWS}; i++) {
    const row = document.createElement("div");
    row.setAttribute("role", "row");
    const cell = document.createElement("div");
    cell.setAttribute("role", "gridcell");
    cell.textContent = "Listing " + i;
    row.appendChild(cell);
    const host = document.createElement("span"); // for example a web component host
    host.style.display = "contents";
    for (let j = 0; j < ${TEXT_NODES_PER_HOST}; j++) {
      host.appendChild(document.createTextNode(" "));
      if (j % 7 === 0) host.appendChild(document.createElement("i"));
    }
    row.appendChild(host);
    grid.appendChild(row);
  }
  window.timeDomOps = (n) => {
    const work = document.getElementById("work");
    const t0 = performance.now();
    for (let i = 0; i < n / 2; i++) {
      const d = document.createElement("div");
      work.appendChild(d);
      work.removeChild(d);
    }
    return performance.now() - t0;
  };
</script></body></html>`;

const browser = await chromium.launch();
async function measure(label, trigger) {
  const page = await browser.newPage();
  await page.setContent(html);
  const before = await page.evaluate((n) => window.timeDomOps(n), DOM_OPS);
  await trigger(page);
  const after = await page.evaluate((n) => window.timeDomOps(n), DOM_OPS);
  await page.close();
  console.log(label, { domOpsMsBefore: +before.toFixed(1), domOpsMsAfter: +after.toFixed(1) });
}
await measure("10 x getByRole('row', { name }).isVisible()", async (page) => {
  for (let i = 0; i < 10; i++) await page.getByRole("row", { name: "Listing 5", exact: true }).isVisible();
});
await measure("10 x locator('body').ariaSnapshot()", async (page) => {
  for (let i = 0; i < 10; i++) await page.locator("body").ariaSnapshot();
});
await measure("control: no Playwright query", async () => {});
await browser.close();
```

### Expected behavior

Playwright queries do not change how fast the page's own DOM changes run afterwards.

### Actual behavior

After the queries, the same 3,000 DOM changes in the page are 30 to 90 times slower (two runs):

| After | DOM changes before | DOM changes after |
| --- | --- | --- |
| 10 × `getByRole('row', { name }).isVisible()` | 0.6–1.1 ms | 27.9–46.8 ms |
| 10 × `locator('body').ariaSnapshot()` | 1.0–1.2 ms | 73.3–90.7 ms |
| no Playwright query (control) | 0.7–1.0 ms | 0.5–0.7 ms |

The cost grows with each query, and it stays until a garbage collection.

### Cause (as far as we can tell)

`isVisibleTextNode()` creates a new `Range` on each call and drops it:

```ts
export function isVisibleTextNode(node: Text) {
  const range = node.ownerDocument.createRange();
  range.selectNode(node);
  const rect = range.getBoundingClientRect();
  return rect.width > 0 && rect.height > 0;
}
```

It runs for each text child of an element with `display: contents` (through `isElementHiddenForAria()` and `computeBox()`), so role queries, accessible names, `toBeVisible()`, and aria snapshots (also the snapshot that a failed `expect` poll takes) all make Ranges. A dropped `Range` stays attached to its document until a GC, and the browser must update every attached Range on each node removal, move, or text change. With the 4,200 text nodes above, each query adds about 4,200 live Ranges.

In our app (web components whose host has `display: contents`, about 35 whitespace text nodes in each row menu, 120 rows), an `expect(...).toBeVisible()` that waited for a grid to render made the render itself take seconds: the client render alone takes 0.23 s, but with Playwright waiting on it, the time from the click to the heading was 10–28 s at 4× CPU throttling. Each failed poll added more Ranges, which made the next DOM changes slower again.

### Possible fix

Reuse one `Range` for each document. With this change, the repro shows no slowdown (DOM changes after the queries: 0.6–0.9 ms), and in our app the click-to-heading time at 4× CPU throttling went from 10–28 s to 1.4–3.6 s:

```ts
const visibleTextNodeRanges = new WeakMap<Document, Range>();

export function isVisibleTextNode(node: Text) {
  let range = visibleTextNodeRanges.get(node.ownerDocument);
  if (!range) {
    range = node.ownerDocument.createRange();
    visibleTextNodeRanges.set(node.ownerDocument, range);
  }
  range.selectNode(node);
  const rect = range.getBoundingClientRect();
  return rect.width > 0 && rect.height > 0;
}
```

(`range.detach()` does not help: it is a no-op in current browsers.)

### Environment

- OS: macOS 26.3.1 (Apple Silicon)
- Node.js: 24.17.0
- Browser: Chromium 153.0.8010.12 (the build that Playwright 1.63.0 installs), headless
- Playwright: playwright-core 1.63.0 (we saw the same in `@playwright/test` 1.63.0)

