All three tests pass: the same array means the same thing in the config's `use`, in `test.use()` and in `browser.newContext()`.

[testOptions.httpCredentials](https://playwright.dev/docs/api/class-testoptions#test-options-http-credentials):
"Pass an array to use different credentials for different origins. The first entry that matches the request origin is
used, and entries with no origin match any request."

[Release notes 1.63](https://playwright.dev/docs/release-notes#browser-and-context): "httpCredentials now also accepts
an array of credentials."

### Actual behavior

In `test.use()` the fixture receives only the first object of the array, so the second origin gets no credentials
(401). The same array works in the config's `use` and in `browser.newContext()`. TypeScript accepts the `test.use()`
form without an error.

```
Running 3 tests using 1 worker

  ✓  1 credentials.spec.ts:29:5 › array in the config use: both origins sign in (80ms)
  ✓  2 credentials.spec.ts:34:5 › array in browser.newContext: both origins sign in (80ms)
httpCredentials fixture value: {"username":"alice","password":"alice-pass","origin":"http://localhost:5701"}
  ✘  3 credentials.spec.ts:45:7 › array in test.use › both origins sign in (66ms)


  1) credentials.spec.ts:45:7 › array in test.use › both origins sign in ───────────────────────────

    Error: expect(received).toBe(expected) // Object.is equality

    Expected: 200
    Received: 401

      46 |     console.log('httpCredentials fixture value:', JSON.stringify(httpCredentials));
      47 |     expect((await page.goto(A))!.status()).toBe(200);
    > 48 |     expect((await page.goto(B))!.status()).toBe(200);
         |                                            ^
      49 |   });
      50 | });
      51 |
        at /path/to/repro/credentials.spec.ts:48:44

    Error Context: test-results/credentials-array-in-test-use-both-origins-sign-in/error-context.md

    Error Context: test-results/credentials-array-in-test-use-both-origins-sign-in/error-context.md

  1 failed
    credentials.spec.ts:45:7 › array in test.use › both origins sign in ────────────────────────────
  2 passed (720ms)
```

### Additional context

**Notes:** `test.use()` values go through `isFixtureTuple()` (`Array.isArray(value) && typeof value[1] === "object"`),
so `[a, b]` is read as `[value, fixtureOptions]`; the config's `use` wraps each override as `[value, options]` first,
which is why it works there. That is the documented "Array as an option value" rule for custom options
([test-fixtures#fixtures-options](https://playwright.dev/docs/test-fixtures#fixtures-options); #32667, #38118), but
`httpCredentials` is a built-in option, its own docs say "Pass an array" with no mention of the extra wrapping, and the
mistake fails silently. Workaround (type-checks and passes):
`test.use({ httpCredentials: [credentials, { scope: 'test' }] })`. Either `test.use()` could treat the array as the
value for this built-in option, or the `httpCredentials` docs could show the wrapped form for `test.use()`.

