query:
[Bug]: page.fill() on <input type=datetime-local> throws Malformed value for valid strings with seconds

### Version

1.65.0-next (main)

### Steps to reproduce

```js
const { chromium } = require('playwright');

(async () => {
  const browser = await chromium.launch();
  const page = await browser.newPage();
  await page.setContent('<input type="datetime-local">');

  // Standard ISO timestamp (e.g. from new Date().toISOString().slice(0, 19))
  await page.fill('input', '2026-10-14T12:00:00');

  await browser.close();
})();
```

### Expected behavior

`page.fill()` accepts `'2026-10-14T12:00:00'`. The browser parses it as a valid local date-time string, sets the element value, and per the WHATWG HTML specification (§4.10.5.1.10) canonicalizes it to `'2026-10-14T12:00'` by omitting seconds when zero.

### Actual behavior

`page.fill()` throws:
```
page.fill: Error: Malformed value
```

### Additional context

Follow-up to #43328 / commit 65f80bbc9 (#43343), which handled range input re-serialization.

In `packages/injected/src/injectedScript.ts`:
```ts
const accepted = type === 'range' ? Number(input.value) === Number(value) : input.value === value;
if (!accepted)
  throw this.createStacklessError('Malformed value');
```

Per WHATWG HTML §4.10.5.1.10 (The datetime-local state):
> "A normalized local date and time string is a valid local date and time string representing the given date and time, with the year part having at least four digits, and with the seconds and fractional seconds parts omitted if the seconds and fractional seconds are zero, unless the step attribute requires seconds or fractional seconds to be present."

When assigning `'2026-10-14T12:00:00'` to `<input type="datetime-local">`, the browser accepts the value and normalizes it to `'2026-10-14T12:00'`. The strict equality check `input.value === value` fails and raises `Malformed value`.

### Environment

- Playwright: 1.65.0-next
- Browsers: Chromium, Firefox
- OS: All platforms

